---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import HingeTool from "../tools/HingeTool";
---

<ToolPage tool="hinge">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        A field of slits cut into a flat panel so that it rolls up. Set the panel, pick a pattern, and the tool works
        out how far <strong class="text-slate-300">every row has to twist</strong> to reach the radius you want — and
        whether the material between the slits will survive it.
    </Fragment>

    <HingeTool client:only="react">
        <div slot="fallback" class="flex h-[100dvh] flex-col bg-[#08090d]">
            <div class="h-12 shrink-0 border-b border-white/7"></div>
            <div class="flex min-h-0 flex-1">
                <div class="hidden w-60 shrink-0 border-r border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
                <div class="flex-1"></div>
                <div class="hidden w-76 shrink-0 border-l border-white/7 xl:block"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="h-7 shrink-0 border-t border-white/7"></div>
        </div>
    </HingeTool>

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">It twists, it does not stretch</h2>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    Bend a solid panel and its outer face has to get longer. Plywood tears at something under two per
                    cent, so 3 mm ply can be persuaded around about a 100 mm radius and no tighter — which is not a
                    hinge, it is a gentle curve.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-2 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    A lattice hinge does not stretch at all. The strips between the slits <strong class="text-slate-300">twist about their own long axis</strong>, and the bend is the sum of all those little twists.
                    Three things follow, and they are the whole tool: the slits have to run
                    <em>parallel to the axis you are bending around</em> or nothing happens; the
                    <strong class="text-slate-300">link</strong> — the uncut material between two slits end to end — is
                    the part that actually breaks; and a hinge with twice as many rows is twice as bendy rather than
                    twice as strong.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="mt-5 grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-3">
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">One exact number</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Most of what you read about living hinges is a rule of thumb. This part is not:
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">twist per row = row spacing ÷ radius</strong>. No material
                        constant, no fudge. Rows 5 mm apart bent to a 40 mm radius turn 5/40 of a radian each — 7.2° —
                        relative to their neighbour. Halve the spacing and you halve what every row is asked for. That
                        is why row spacing, not slit length, is the control that decides how tightly a panel rolls.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 110ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">And one that is a guess</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        The strain figure turns that twist into a prediction: a link of a given length, twisted through
                        a given angle in a sheet of a given thickness, shears by about so much. Where the warning
                        starts — 3.5 % — is a rule of thumb and nothing more. Plywood in rolling shear across the
                        plies, acrylic, MDF and a hardwood veneer all give up at different points, and the same board
                        behaves differently damp. Treat it as a place to start arguing, not an answer.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 220ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">The kerf eats the link</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Both ends of a link are slit ends, so the beam takes a full kerf out of it. A 5 mm link in a
                        0.15 mm kerf is really 4.85 mm of material — barely worth mentioning. A 1 mm link is 0.85 mm,
                        which is a fifth of the part that has to hold. The status bar shows what is left after the
                        beam, not what you typed.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Three patterns, and why you would pick each</h2>
                <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-4 text-sm leading-relaxed sm:grid-cols-3">
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Straight</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            Plain slits in a brick bond. Quickest to cut, stiffest of the three, and the one that
                            cracks first — a slit ends in a point, and a point is where all the stress in the link
                            goes.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">Wave</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            The same field with each slit running as one S. A longer cut takes the same bend more
                            gently, and the material at the end of a slit is never asked to turn a corner. It is also
                            the one that looks deliberate rather than perforated.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <dt class="font-semibold text-slate-200">T-ends</dt>
                        <dd class="mt-1 text-slate-400">
                            Straight slits with a short bar across each end, spreading the stress along a line instead
                            of piling it on a point. Costs a little stiffness and buys a hinge that survives being
                            opened twice. The bar is left off any end that runs off the edge, which would cut the
                            corner of the panel clean off.
                        </dd>
                    </div>
                </dl>
            </div>

            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-2">
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Flat ends, no border</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">Flat ends</strong> keep the two ends of the bend uncut, so the
                        hinge is a band across the middle — which is what a curved box lid is: two flat ends to glue
                        into the sides, and the curve between them. A <strong class="text-slate-300">border</strong>
                        along the other two edges is almost always a mistake: it is stiffer than everything inside it,
                        so the panel bends around the border rather than with it. Leave it at 0 unless something has to
                        be screwed to that edge.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Cut the strip first</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        There is a <em>test strip</em> preset for a reason. Forty by sixty millimetres costs a minute
                        and a scrap, and it answers the only question that matters — whether <em>this</em> sheet, at
                        <em>this</em> pattern, reaches the radius you need without a link letting go. A hinge that
                        fails does not fail gracefully: it fails at one link, and then at the next.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="reveal mt-5 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-500">
                The rows are rounded to fit the panel exactly rather than leaving a stub at one edge, and so are the
                slits along each row — the link is held at what you set, because it is the part carrying the load, and
                the slit length gives way to make the arithmetic come out. Everything is a cut, in red; there is
                nothing to engrave on a hinge.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
